as i said a few videos ago  ubisoft announced the expansion into  what they are calling  a division universe where they've been  working on additional ways to introduce  the division franchise  to even more gamers and this has set  them up to move on a trajectory that  will include more titles  and more transmedia content such as the  division mobile game  so i said i'd stop speculating about  what was to come with these new  division-based games but  i guess i lied i can't help myself but  this time i feel that i have a little  more to back up what i'm hoping to see  it isn't the first time that the  divisions talked about including the  mobile side of gaming  those of you have been around playing  the division for a while will remember  or have read about the companion app  that was originally planned to be  released at the same time as the  division one  this would allow mobile gamers to  interact with friends playing the main  game  the player on the app would be the one  manning a support drone  that could rain death from above or buff  the armor of teammates  or alternatively reduce the armor of  hostiles  however this was canned before release  due to the imbalance it created  the developers confirmed that it  compromised the team-based competitive  gameplay  to be fair i was never surprised by this  as much as i loved the idea behind it  i could imagine the disadvantage and  balancing issues this would have created  not to mention the number of technical  difficulties this would have included in  the long run  regardless ubisoft has been no stranger  to mobile games  in recent years they've released  spin-offs based on assassin's creed  rabbids just dance and trials  they've also featured the tom clancy  brand with tom clancy's elite squad  a crossover title that features heroes  from rainbow six  ghost recon the division splinter cell  and hawks franchises  technically api is an acronym for  application  programming interface this basically  means a software interface that allows  two applications to communicate with  each other  in gaming this allows the game and  software to interact with one another  and share rules settings specifications  and data it's only natural to think that  the mobile game or app  could simply be a way to tie all the  games together a number of you may be  aware of websites like the division  tracker  where your gaming stats over time are  rounded up showing things like total pve  kills  pvp kills time played type of enemy  killed  most used weapon types and much much  more  it would be pretty cool to mess around  with loadouts and gear too and it could  possibly even link the main game and  heartlands together somehow  while i like the idea of this type of  companion app i'm fairly certain this  isn't the direction they're going to  take  if we remember back to the only  information they've given us so far  ubisoft stated that they've been  exploring additional ways to introduce  the division to even more players  that games like heartland is to offer a  whole new experience  they are wanting to appeal to new  audiences and different games  to try and spread the name and gain a  larger portion of the industry market  share  whether you're familiar with this term  or not you've likely heard of the game  pokemon go augmented reality gaming is  the integration of game visual and audio  content with the user's environment in  real time  more often than not these games  superimpose a pre-created environment  on top of the user's actual environment  with pokemon go the game uses your  phone's gps and clock to detect where  and when you are in the game and make  the pokemon appear  around you as you move around different  and more types of pokemon will appear  depending on where you are  and what time it is this mix of a game  and real world interacting  is known as augmented reality  no i don't want to run around catching  pokemon this is probably just the most  well-known version of an ar game  and was the easiest way to explain it  but there is another game called ingress  that actually came out before pokemon go  similar to pokemon go users are expected  to walk around the real world and visit  nearby landmarks and parks  but there is a little more involved with  ingress in fact  ingress players often joke about pokemon  go as being the kitty version of ingress  it doesn't have any of the cute little  creatures to capture and has a much  darker story behind it  of which i won't go into right now but  the players are to hack  portals and link them together in an  effort to conquer as much of the map as  you can for your faction  link three portals and everything in  between becomes your faction's turf  players are able to gain experience and  level up which opens access to more  powerful items that can be collected  the defense of these portals comes down  to the level of the gear and weapons  that's placed on them  and the more high-level players who've  placed gear on them the more difficult  it is to take them down  there is a real-time world map that  constantly updates the status of how  each faction is doing  and this can be narrowed down to country  or city  this led to very tight communities we're  in a number of instances that i can  recall  and this was a few years ago players or  agents as the golden game  would travel to different countries to  assist fellow members and regaining  control of the region  there are actually a surprising number  of events that are held all over the  world  i remember a friend of mine having a  plane ticket bought for her by her  faction  located in a couple of neighbouring  countries so that she could travel to a  remote island  and capture a portal that was under  enemy control all  so that they would stop linking to it  both ingress and pokemon go have certain  real world locations  that held significance within the game  gyms for pokemon  and portals for ingress these were all  based on real world landmarks that early  players of the game had submitted  and had approved i actually think i  still have a few out there and some of  them were  pretty ridiculous to be fair i think i  managed to get a park bench and a  rubbish bin accepted at one point  anyway what i'm trying to say is that  ingress already has the bones of what a  decent ar division game could be  the landmarks could represent well  landmarks  capturing these landmarks within a  certain area would gain control of the  region  for your faction the factions could be  shd versus rogues in the most simple  form but there's no reason why other  groups couldn't be included  think of a worldwide battle for control  there could be certain weapons and  skills that could be collected  that could be used in defending or  attacking enemy controlled landmarks  now i'm not just talking about this  because i think it would be cool and  that i was borderline obsessed with  ingress when it first came out  there have been a few little flags that  have had me thinking down this path  firstly several years ago in 2017  the ceo of ubisoft had a number of  interviews where he praised what niantic  and nintendo were able to achieve and  produce with pokemon go  he went on to talk about how ubisoft 2  is working on a product  using augmented reality but he didn't  really go into too much detail than that  however fast forward to just before the  release of the division 2  ubisoft released something called echo's  augmented reality experience  we call them echoes  echoes are all around you they recount  what happened near your grocery store  going somewhere no no no we're not no  we're not  your school one two  what's happening and even your local gas  station  be quick okay good it's working  discovering echo will unlock rewards  rewards that in a few weeks  will help ensure your survival  [Music]  in this short introduction to the game  users were able to explore around the  neighborhood  and find echoes these would provide a  small amount of story towards the main  game  that will be coming out in a few weeks  but also some in-game cosmetics  this app or game had very little content  and didn't take long to acquire all  available collectibles  but it didn't really feel like it was  just a marketing piece for the division  two  it wasn't even available in all  countries it felt like more of a test of  some of their basic framework  to be utilized more in depth later on  like they were just toying with the idea  at that stage  perhaps for a future mobile game they  had in mind  [Music]  so what do you think the division  augmented reality would certainly bring  a different type of gamer  while still being interesting to a  number of us who enjoy the main game  i know i could certainly use a reason to  get off my ass for a change and get some  exercise  but truth be told i actually hope it  doesn't go down this path  if it's anything like ingress i'll  probably end up being obsessively  competitive again  and i certainly wouldn't have the time  to be making content for youtube anymore  or spending time with my family or  turning up to work  or eating but my region would definitely  be well defended  regardless we'll hopefully be finding  out more about their plans for the  division and the various games they're  working on for the franchise at ubisoft  forward or e3  in june thank you very much for  listening to yet another edition of  ngn attempting to create gaming ideas  for the division franchise  catch you in the next one cheers  [Music]  foreign

